Share model data with future interpreters

Introduce runtime signatures and dynamic structures so callers can
inspect and construct Eqlog data without generating Rust or running
saturation. Keep old/new partitions, union-find state, and membership
relations in the same storage types used by compiled models. Select
relation arities and column orders at runtime.

Preserve element IDs, equality aliases, and raw relation rows through
conversion. Share prefix-tree nodes where index layouts agree and copy
union-find vectors deeply. Rebuild compiled lookup indices on import
without resolving pending equalities or executing rules.

Keep storage access behind a doc-hidden interface for generated code so
evaluation bookkeeping stays out of the public model API.
Describe functions and their results directly in API docs.
Use the compiler's type terminology throughout the dynamic API.
Keep its public operations analogous to compiled models.
Expose Model and signature descriptors at the runtime crate root.
Borrow compiled signatures from a OnceLock while sharing runtime-created
signatures through Arc.
Match compiled membership and morphism checks, function evaluation and
definition, enum construction and case inspection, and equality queries.

Test descriptor validation, copy independence, API behavior against
compiled models, and continued evaluation after conversion through
public APIs.
